<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Preah Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1191">1191</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Nagara Jayasri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Preah Khan is a Buddhist temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, built in the 12th century for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> to honour his father, on the site of his victory over the invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a> in 1181. Its main image, the bodhisattva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avalokitesvara">Avalokiteśvara</a> in the form of the king's father, was dedicated in 1191, and its foundation stela records an institution that combined city, temple and Buddhist university, with 97,840 attendants and servants, among them 1000 dancers and 1000 teachers. Royal support ended around the 15th century and the complex slowly declined. Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> it has been left largely unrestored, and since 1991 the World Monuments Fund has maintained it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/preah-khan.jpg" alt="Preah Khan" width="330" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Preah_Khan,_Angkor,_Camboya,_2013-08-17,_DD_26.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
